   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the estates, powers and trusts law, in relation to exempt assets for the benefit of the family of a decedent",
    "basePrintNo": "S7201",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7201-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §5-3.1, EPT L",
    "lawSection": "Estates, Powers and Trusts Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7201\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the estates, powers and trusts law, in relation to\nexempt assets for the benefit of the family of a decedent\n\n\n PURPOSE OF BILL :\nThis section provides for certain personal property to be exempt from\nthe provisions of a will or intestate distribution and to be\nautomatically set off to the decedent's surviving spouse to provide\nsupport for him or her and the children during the period of estate\nadministration. In the event there is no surviving spouse, the exempt\nproperty is set off to the decedent's children who are under 21.\n\nThis bill amends EPTL §5-3.1 by expanding some of the articles of\npersonal property to be included, deleting some of the items that were\npreviously included and increasing the value of all of the items\nreferenced therein.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nThe provisions of this section were last revised and updated in 1992\nat the recommendation of the EPTL-SCPA Advisory Committee. In the past\n15 years, inflation has caused the price of the exempt items to rise\nthus reducing the value of the automobile, furniture and other items\nthe surviving spouse or family may receive.  The total potential set\noff was increased in 1992 from $26,150 to $56,000. The present\nproposal would increase it again to $92,500.00.\n\nIt should be noted this maximum limit is often never reached and is\nachieved only if the decedent owned the right combination of personal\nproperty in the amounts set forth in each subsection of EPTL §5-3.1.\nFor example, if decedent did not own any items referred to in (a)(3),\nthe $20,000 of value allowed for that item would not go to the family,\nthus reducing the potential total of exempt property to $72,500.00.\n\nA short summary of the monetary changes is as follows:\n\n1. Household items, etc. was changed from $10,000 to $20,000.\n\n2. Books, computers, etc. was changed from $1,000 to 2,500.\n\n3. Farm animals and tractor were changed from $15,000 to $20,000.\n\n4. The automobile was changed from $15,000 to $25,000.\n\n5. Money (cash equivalents) was changed from $15,000 to $25,000.\n\nThe bill also provides that a specific legatee of any of the specified\nitems will receive any excess paid by the spouse or appropriate\nchildren over and above the value of such item. The bill also deals\nwith cash passing to a minor. The first $10,000 will be distributed to\na minor child under SCPA §2220 without the need for a guardian.  If\nthe amount actually due the minor is over $10,000, the overage would\nbe released upon their majority or through a guardianship proceeding.\nObviously a child between 18 and 21 years of age could receive more\nthan $10,000. The bill also provides the Court with the power to issue\ndocumentation to facilitate the transfer of assets under this section.\nPresently, there is no statutory authority for this.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nAll of the changes are necessary in light of the changes in our\nsociety since the last amendment to the statute which was done over\nfifteen years ago. Today, most people not only have a home computer\nbut they also have all of the accompanying hardware, home music and\nvideo libraries. We also factored in the rising value of furniture.\nIn addition, the value of automobiles, farm machinery, and tractors\nalone require that the respective maximum values be raised to coincide\nwith these everyday items.\n\nThe bill eliminates \"or other personal property\" and adds language to\ninclude cash and cash equivalents, so that this section gives the\nsurviving spouse or children the means to pay their immediate expenses\nduring the settlement of the estate which was probably the original\nintent of the section. Liquid assets, rather than personal property,\nis the intent of this subsection.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nThe increased dollar amounts which could be set off to spouse and\nchildren may result in a decease in filings of administration\npetitions and commensurate reduction in the time of court personnel to\nreview such filings. The loss of filing fees which might have been\nreceived wilt be more than offset by the savings of time for court\npersonnel.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nJanuary 1st next succeeding the date it becomes law.\n",
